  "restoration_note": "Teaching 41 lays out the structural arc of regeneration as three Great Days (descent of the divine humanity into the natural; reigning of the ordered states in the new aeon; full unveiling of the Father with his hidden storehouses) and two deaths (the long mingling of light with the archons of darkness, ended in the great fire of consummation; and the second, irreversible death of those separated from the Light, the living breath, and the living Soul). The vast majority of gaps are letter-level completions or formulaic repetitions ('new aeon,' 'image of heaven,' 'eternity of eternities,' 'Father of the Greatness'), which I confirmed against editor proposals. Substantive lacunae in §§2326–2330 yielded to parallel formulas (image-of-heaven, choosing-out, gushing life of the soul, opened eyes). The damaged stretch in §§2361–2363 describing the binding of the sinners and the figure of 'the woman' (likely Hyle/Enthumesis) lacks securable anchors and is left unrestorable. The Coptic ⲁⲣⲭⲱⲛ at {49}, ⲧⲩⲭⲁⲩⲉ at {50}, ⲃⲁϣⲟⲩ at {51}, and ⲛⲓϩⲉ ⲉⲧⲁⲛⲍ̄ at {52} are all secured by the second-death triad of separations from glory, breath, and Soul.",
